WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.542
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, it turns out

00:00:01.542 --> 00:00:03.270
that switching to
microservices is not

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.490
that easy without a
plan and a process,

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.370
so you need to keep watching to
learn more about the migration

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.448
process.

00:00:09.448 --> 00:00:13.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.170
CARTER MORGAN: The
microservices-based

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.840
architecture is beneficial
due to its loosely coupled

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.340
components that can
be independently

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.290
tested and deployed.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.390
In theory, the smaller and
simpler a component is,

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.292
the easier it is to
maintain and deploy.

00:00:31.292 --> 00:00:33.250
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And
each independent service

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.222
can be implemented in different
languages and frameworks

00:00:36.222 --> 00:00:38.680
so you can use the right tool
for the particular job you're

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:39.222
trying to do.

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:40.597
CARTER MORGAN:
Each component can

00:00:40.597 --> 00:00:42.340
be managed by a
different team, reducing

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.260
dependencies between teams.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.090
This clear boundary
between services

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.460
allows a team to more
easily design for failure,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.960
and it becomes
easier determine what

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.928
to do if a service is down.

00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:53.470
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
But does this mean

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.770
microservices are as perfect
as a homemade souffle?

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:57.760
Well, no.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.100
You see, with
monolithic platforms,

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.880
every logical component can
speak to every other component

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.710
pretty much by default
because they're

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.300
all part of the same whole.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.280
But with microservices,
even though

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.970
each individual component is
simpler and easier to manage,

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.630
how those services work together
to communicate and to behave

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.560
as a system is more complicated
and possibly slower due

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.210
to the added network latency.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.290
CARTER MORGAN: Still,
many, including Google,

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:25.960
has been doing this
for many years.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.120
Many believe that
microservices are a big win

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.350
overall for most organizations.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.400
But if you don't design your
microservices correctly,

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.590
you may end up with a
distributed monolith.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.240
And that's even worse
than the monolith

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.680
you had in the first place.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.793
It can take months
to actually migrate.

00:01:40.793 --> 00:01:42.460
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So we definitely know

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.330
it's not going to be simple.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.210
But to help simplify
that process--

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:47.980
and because with
any recipe, it's

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.050
important to know what the end
result is going to look like--

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.650
let's map the path from
a monolithic, on-premise

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.570
application to an
application that's

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.580
fully hosted on
Google Cloud Platform

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.923
and built with microservices.

00:01:58.923 --> 00:02:00.340
CARTER MORGAN: If
you have already

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.170
migrated your
monolithic application

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.680
as is into the cloud, then
the same steps we discuss here

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.680
can be applied to create
microservices architecture.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.990
For most e-commerce
platforms, the starting point

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.565
is a monolithic
application on premises.

00:02:13.565 --> 00:02:15.940
Your platform will probably
have load balancers to handle

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.920
incoming requests from the web.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.620
These requests will be routed
to your application servers.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.050
These application servers are
responsible for processing

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.600
the requests by utilizing
components like cache,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.700
database, search, et cetera.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.960
Additionally, your app
servers may send requests

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.480
to other back-end systems.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.610
Obviously, this can't
be fully representative

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.470
of your current
system, but it should

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.750
serve as an example
that's similar in theory

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.030
to yours so you can migrate
it towards our target

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:40.845
architecture.

00:02:40.845 --> 00:02:42.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Now let's talk

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.230
about the end state, what
we are trying to achieve.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.530
We definitely want to achieve
the same functionality,

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.360
as Carter mentioned,
in the monolith,

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.730
except now, instead
of running a monolith,

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.090
we would break that up into
individual microservices

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.850
running in portable,
deployable units of code called

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.040
containers, using
Google Kubernetes

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.410
Engine, a platform
that handles scaling,

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:05.110
hosting, and
deploying containers.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.690
In this architecture,
each microservice

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.910
runs in its own
containers and makes

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.060
call to the back-end system
through a secure network

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.073
connection.

00:03:14.073 --> 00:03:16.240
We are still routing internet
traffic through a load

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.850
balancer, but now
the traffic is routed

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.100
into separate microservices.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.880
This lets us update version
of individual microservices

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.120
while only minimally
affecting the other services.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.000
And in our target
architecture, the microservices

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.210
may interact with a number
of other GCP products.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.630
Cloud Storage, Cloud
SQL, and Cloud Pub/Sub

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.900
are common GCP products for
e-commerce applications.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.657
So Carter, how do
we make this happen?

00:03:42.657 --> 00:03:44.740
CARTER MORGAN: One of the
most important decisions

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.600
you must make early
during this migration

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.630
is how to handle communication
between the new microservices

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:53.027
hosted on GKE and your
legacy system on premises.

00:03:53.027 --> 00:03:54.610
Since we're migrating
in stages, there

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.068
will be large
periods of time where

00:03:56.068 --> 00:03:59.350
components of your platform
will be living in both GKE

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.290
and on premises.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.720
Now, there are two
main solutions,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.160
and they can co-exist--

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.800
API-based communication
or communication

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.005
based on a private
network connection.

00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:10.880
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: In
the API-based solution,

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.060
you use an API management
solution, such as Apigee,

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.760
as a proxy between
the two environments.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:19.970
This gives you a precise
control over what portions

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:24.330
of your legacy system you
expose and how you expose them.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.480
It also lets you
seamlessly refactor

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.400
the implementation of an API--

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.910
that is, moving from a legacy
service to a microservice--

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.212
without impacting the
consumers of the API.

00:04:35.212 --> 00:04:37.670
CARTER MORGAN: In a solution
based on private connectivity,

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.820
you connect your GCP and
on-premise environments

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.960
using a private
network connection.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.210
The microservices communicate
with your legacy system

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.770
over this connection.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:49.520
You can set up IPsec-based
VPN tunnels with Cloud VPN.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.440
For larger bandwidth,
high availability,

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.990
and low latency needs,
Cloud Interconnect

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:55.610
is a better option.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now
let's compare and contrast

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.630
the two communication options.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:01.520
Compared to private
connectivity,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.270
an API-based solution is
implemented by the application

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:08.570
teams and requires a greater
integration with the legacy

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:12.950
application from the get go,
so it is harder to set up

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:16.670
but provides more management
options in the long run.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:18.500
On the other hand,
a solution based

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.990
on Cloud VPN or
Cloud Interconnect

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:23.390
will be implemented
by a networking team

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:26.390
and initially requires less
integration with the legacy

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:29.510
application, but it does
not provide any added

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:30.707
value in the long term.

00:05:30.707 --> 00:05:33.290
CARTER MORGAN: Now that we know
more about these communication

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:35.390
options, we can combine
the two approaches

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:36.960
and use them to our benefit.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.780
For example, we
can use Apigee only

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.660
for the public APIs in front
of the microservice and legacy

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.940
systems but not between
them, and Cloud VPN or cloud

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.360
internet for the communication
between the microservice

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.800
and legacy systems.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.840
This option combines the
benefits of the two approaches

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.338
but is more complex to manage.

00:05:53.338 --> 00:05:55.130
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: One
of the other choices

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.680
we have to make
when migrating is

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.140
which GCP technologies to use.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:04.220
For example, do you want to use
Cloud Bigtable to store data,

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.590
or do you want to
keep using some

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:09.170
of your current technologies
because they're still

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.720
relevant to you or because
the cost of moving them

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.070
is too high?

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.600
You should think
about these choices

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.837
as a part of the
migration process.

00:06:16.837 --> 00:06:18.170
CARTER MORGAN: Here's the point.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:22.470
This is a complex process
that won't happen overnight.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:23.980
So any planning
you do beforehand,

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:25.910
like how will my
services communicate,

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:29.240
how will I manage data, what
should I migrate first--

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:32.730
any of this goes to help make
sure the process goes smoothly.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.730
For more information about
alternative migration

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:36.560
approaches, check
out the article

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:37.768
written by Marco [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:37.768 --> 00:06:39.560
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, there you have it.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.630
If you are in the process
of migrating a monolith

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.660
to microservices, then you got
a small taste of the process

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:45.260
here.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.000
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.550
we will be discussing
the migration in stages.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:51.830
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.210
If you liked this
video and would

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.010
like to see more
such content, don't

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:56.930
forget to like and subscribe.

